# Changes Log - Substack-Library

This file is the handoff record for the repository. It tells any person or any
assistant (Claude Code, Codex, ChatGPT, or a human) what was done, why it was
done, and what to do next. Read this file first before you start work.

## How to read this file

- "Current state" is the only section that is rewritten. It always shows the
  present position of the project. Trust it before you trust the entries.
- "Entries" is append-only and newest first. Do not edit or delete old entries.
  If an old entry became wrong, write a new entry that corrects it.
- Git history records the code. This file records the intent, the decisions,
  and the point to continue from. Do not repeat the diff here.

## How to add an entry

Add a new entry at the top of the "Entries" section. Use this template. Use
absolute dates (YYYY-MM-DD), not "today" or "last week". Keep the sentences
short and plain.

    ### YYYY-MM-DD - <short title>

    **What changed:** <the change, in one or two sentences>
    **Why:** <the reason, and the option that was rejected if there was one>
    **Files:** <the files that were touched>
    **Verification:** <the command that was run and its result, or "not run">
    **State after:** <what works now and what does not>
    **Next up:** <the immediate next step>
    **Open questions:** <what is undecided, or "none">

Then update the "Current state" section to match.

